{"subjectId":"java","id":"enums","title":"Enums","group":"Advanced Types","order":33,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">An <code>enum</code> is a class with a fixed, named set of instances. It's type-safe, can carry fields and behaviour, and works in <code>switch</code>, <code>EnumMap</code> and <code>EnumSet</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Enum</strong> — a special class whose instances are exactly the listed constants, created once when the class loads. Each constant is a <code>public static final</code> instance; every enum implicitly extends <code>java.lang.Enum</code>.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list\"></i>Built-in methods</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    enum Day { MON, TUE, WED, THU, FRI, SAT, SUN }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Day d = Day.WED;\n        System.out.println(d + \" \" + d.name() + \" \" + d.ordinal());\n        System.out.println(Day.valueOf(\"FRI\").compareTo(Day.MON));  // by ordinal\n        System.out.println(Day.values().length);\n        System.out.println(d == Day.WED);          // safe: constants are singletons\n        try {\n            Day.valueOf(\"Funday\");\n        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {\n            System.out.println(e.getMessage());\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>WED WED 2\n4\n7\ntrue\nNo enum constant Main.Day.Funday</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Member</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>values()</code></td><td>Array of all constants in declaration order (a fresh copy each call)</td></tr><tr><td><code>valueOf(String)</code></td><td>Constant by exact name, else <code>IllegalArgumentException</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>name()</code> / <code>toString()</code></td><td>Declared name (<code>toString</code> can be overridden, <code>name</code> cannot)</td></tr><tr><td><code>ordinal()</code></td><td>0-based position — don't persist it; reordering constants changes it</td></tr><tr><td><code>compareTo</code></td><td>Compares by ordinal; enums are <code>Comparable</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-settings\"></i>Fields, constructors and methods</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    enum Planet {\n        MERCURY(3.303e+23, 2.4397e6),\n        EARTH(5.976e+24, 6.37814e6);\n\n        private final double mass, radius;            // per-constant data\n        Planet(double mass, double radius) {          // constructor is implicitly private\n            this.mass = mass;\n            this.radius = radius;\n        }\n        double surfaceGravity() {\n            return 6.67300E-11 * mass / (radius * radius);\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        for (Planet p : Planet.values())\n            System.out.printf(\"%s %.2f%n\", p, p.surfaceGravity());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>MERCURY 3.70\nEARTH 9.80</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-puzzle\"></i>Constant-specific bodies</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    enum Op {\n        ADD(\"+\") { int apply(int a, int b) { return a + b; } },\n        MUL(\"*\") { int apply(int a, int b) { return a * b; } };\n\n        final String symbol;\n        Op(String symbol) { this.symbol = symbol; }\n        abstract int apply(int a, int b);            // each constant must implement it\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        for (Op op : Op.values())\n            System.out.println(\"6 \" + op.symbol + \" 7 = \" + op.apply(6, 7));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>6 + 7 = 13\n6 * 7 = 42</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-switch-horizontal\"></i>Switch over an enum</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    enum Level { LOW, MEDIUM, HIGH }\n\n    static String action(Level l) {\n        return switch (l) {             // exhaustive: no default needed\n            case LOW -&gt; \"ignore\";\n            case MEDIUM -&gt; \"log\";\n            case HIGH -&gt; \"page on-call\";\n        };\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        for (Level l : Level.values()) System.out.println(l + \" -&gt; \" + action(l));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>LOW -&gt; ignore\nMEDIUM -&gt; log\nHIGH -&gt; page on-call</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>In a <code>case</code> label you write the bare constant <code>LOW</code>, not <code>Level.LOW</code> (qualified names in case labels are only allowed from Java 21). A switch <em>expression</em> over an enum must cover every constant or have a <code>default</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-table\"></i>EnumSet and EnumMap</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    enum Day { MON, TUE, WED, THU, FRI, SAT, SUN }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        EnumSet&lt;Day&gt; weekend = EnumSet.of(Day.SAT, Day.SUN);          // a bit vector inside\n        EnumSet&lt;Day&gt; weekdays = EnumSet.complementOf(weekend);\n        System.out.println(weekdays);\n        System.out.println(EnumSet.range(Day.TUE, Day.THU));\n\n        Map&lt;Day, Integer&gt; hours = new EnumMap&lt;&gt;(Day.class);          // array indexed by ordinal\n        hours.put(Day.FRI, 6);\n        hours.put(Day.MON, 8);\n        System.out.println(hours);                                   // always in declaration order\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[MON, TUE, WED, THU, FRI]\n[TUE, WED, THU]\n{MON=8, FRI=6}</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Use <code>EnumSet</code> instead of bit flags and <code>EnumMap</code> instead of <code>HashMap</code> with enum keys — both are faster, compact, and iterate in declaration order.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-user\"></i>The enum singleton</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    enum Registry {\n        INSTANCE;\n        private int count;\n        int next() { return ++count; }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Registry.INSTANCE.next();\n        System.out.println(Registry.INSTANCE.next());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>2</code></pre></figure>\n<p><em>Effective Java</em> calls a single-element enum the best singleton: the JVM guarantees one instance, it is thread-safe to initialise, and it is safe against reflection and serialization attacks that break classic singletons.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Don't store or persist <code>ordinal()</code> (e.g. in a database). Adding or reordering a constant silently changes every ordinal. Persist <code>name()</code> or an explicit code field instead.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Can an enum extend a class? Implement an interface?</summary><div><p>It cannot extend anything — it already extends <code>java.lang.Enum</code>. It <em>can</em> implement interfaces, which is a common way to make several enums interchangeable.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why is comparing enums with == safe?</summary><div><p>Each constant is created exactly once per class loader and can't be instantiated again (constructors are private; reflection and deserialization are blocked). So identity equals equality, and <code>==</code> is also null-safe.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why can't an enum constructor be public?</summary><div><p>The set of instances is fixed at compile time. Allowing <code>new</code> would break that guarantee, so enum constructors are implicitly <code>private</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}